  "text_md": "![](<11_pg36_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<11_pg36_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n# LAV 508 LAW\n\nWho lukes to the lefte syde, wheune his horse launches. With the lyghte of the sonne men myghtesee his lyvere.\n\nEsguiere ,' Leg. Cathol. p. 154 ;\n\nReliq. Antiq.\n\ni. 7; Davies' Ancient Rites, 1672, p. 130.\n\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 80.\n\nAnd fulleglad, certys, thou schalt bee,\n\nLAUNDE. A plain place in a wood ;\n\nYf that y wylle sufifur the To hulde me a lavour and bason to my honde.\n\nan un-\n\nploughed plain ; a park ; a lawn. \" Saltus, a\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii.38, f. 144.\n\nlawnd,\" Nominale MS.\n\n- (3) A dish composed of a kind of sea-weed well washed and boiled. It is also called laver^ bread, Kennett, MS. Lansd. 1033.\n- (4) Laver lip, a hanging lip. LAVERD. Lord. {A.-S.)\n\n\nNow is Gij to a launde y-go, Wher the dragoun duelled tho.\n\nGy of Warwike, p. 262.\n\nFor to hunt at the hartes in thas hye laundes\n\nIn Glamorgane with glee, thare gladchipe was evere.\n\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 53.\n\nThat tay after thaym ne went To du thayr laverd comandement.\n\nLAUNDER. (1) Any kind of gutter or channel\n\nGuy of Warwick, Middlehill MS. LAVEROCK. The lark. North. See Wright's\n\nfor conveying water. Var. dial.\n\n(2) A washer. Also, to wash. \" Buanditre,\n\nLyric Poetry, pp. 26, 40 ;\n\nReliq. Antiq. i. 86\n\nlaunderer.\" HoUyband's Dictionarie, 1593.\n\n;\n\nLaundring gold, washing it.\n\nWright's Purgatory, p. 55\n\nlaverkes, Beves of\n\n;\n\nLAUNDRE. A laundress. Palsgrave.\n\nHamtoun, p. 138,\n\nSche made many a wondir soune,\n\nLAUP. To leap. Yorksh.\n\nSumtyme liche unto the cok,\n\nLAUREAT. Crowned with laurel. {Lat.) The\n\nSumlyme unto the laverok.\n\nlaureatship at our universities was a degree in grammar, including poetry and rhetoric, so\n\nGower, MS. Sac. Antiq. 134, f. 152.\n\nTyrlery lovpyn, the laverocke songe,\n\ncalled because the person who graduated was\n\nSo meryly pypes the sparow\n\n;\n\npresented with a wreath of laurel.\n\nThe cow brake lose, the rope ran home,\n\nLAUREOLE. Spurge-laurel. {A.-N.)\n\nSyr, God gyve yow good morow.\n\nLAURER. A laurel. Chaucer.\n\nBliss's Bibl. Miscell. p. 54.\n\nLAUS. Loose. {A.-S.)\n\nLAVISH. Rank, as grass, &c. West.\n\nLA-VOLTA. A kind of very active bouncing\n\nLAUTER. The laurel. {A.-N.)\n\nThat worthy was the lauter to have Of poetrie, and the palme to atteyne.\n\nwaltz, formerly much in fashion. The man turned the woman round several times, and then assisted her in making a high spring.\n\nLydgate, MS. Aahmole 39, f. 48.\n\nLAU3T. Caught ; received. {A.-S.)\n\nLeave protestations now, and let us hie To tread lavolta, that is women's walk.\n\nThenne was Marie Joseph bitau5t,\n\nAndhehir in spousaile/awjf.\n\nSoliman and Perseda, p. 214.\n\nCursor Mundi, MS. Coll. Trin. Cantab, f. 67.\n\nLAVY. Lavish : liberal. North. LAW. (1) To give a hare good law, i. e. a good\n\nLAVALTOE. Same as Lavolta, q. v. For lo ! the liveless Jacks lavaltoes take At that sweet musick which themselves do make.\n\nstart before the hounds. It is in very fre-\n\nquent use by boys at play.\n\nBrome*a Songs, ed. 1661, p. 133.\n\n- (2) A hill, or eminence. North.\n- (3) Custom ; manner. See Ellis, ii. 335.\n- (4) Low. North. He wist not that hym was gode. But then he putte doune his hode On knees he fel downe latvc.\n\n\nLAVANDRE. A laundress. \"A tretise for\n\nlavandres,'' Reliq. Antiq. i. 26.\n\nLAVANT. Aland-spring. South.\n\nLAVAS. Lavish, Romeus and Juliet, p. 20.\n\nLAVAST. Uninclosed stubble. Kent.\n\nLAVE. (1) The rest ; the remainder. North.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 55.\n\n- (2) To lade or draw water. Chaucer. Also, to pour, as in Perceval, 2250 ; to wash, Piers Ploughman, p. 273.\n- (3) To gutter, as a candle. Wilts.\n- (4) To hang, or flap down. Hall. LAVE-EARED. Long, or flap-eared. See\n\n\nLAWAND. Bowing ; humbUng.\n\nAnely lawand thameselfe to the sacramenles of haly kyrke, thof it be swa that thay hafebenecumbyrde in syne and with syne alle thaire lyfe tyme.\n\nMS. Lincoln A. 1. 17, f- 229. LAWE. (1) To laugh. Nominale MS.\n\nHawkins, iii. 357\n\nTopsell's Beasts, p. 366 ;\n\n(2) Rough ; violent ; brutal. West.\n\n;\n\nLavelugged, Northumb. HoUoway has lapeared in use in Sussex and Hants.\n\nLAWED. Ignorant. See Laird.\n\nLAWES. The same as Kaims, q. v.\n\nLAWESTE. The lowest. North.\n\nLAVEER. To work a ship against the wind.\n\nAn old sea term. LAVELL. The flap that covers the top of the\n\nLenges all at laysere, and lokes one the wallys\n\nWhare they ware latveate the ledes to assaille.\n\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 79»\n\nwindpipe. Still used in Devon.\n\nLAWFUL-CASE. An interj. of surprize.\n\nLAVENDER. To lay in lavender, to pawn. This is a very common phrase in old plays. \" To lay to pawne, as we say to lay in lavander,\" Florio, p. 27.\n\nLAWGHE. Low. Hampole.\n\nLAWING. (1) Going to law. IJnc.\n\n(2) Lawing of dogs, i. e. cutting out the balls,\n\nor three claws of the fore-feet.\n\nLAVENDREY. Washing. (A.-N.)\n\nLAWLESS-MAN. An outlaw. {A.-S.)\n\nLAVER. (1) The remainder. North.\n\nLAWN. The same as Launde, q. v. LAWNDER. The sliding iron in the fore-part\n\n(2) A cistern, trough, or conduit, to wash in. \" Laver to washe at, lavoyr\" Palsgrave. Also, a basin. See Florio, p. 89 ;\n\nof a plough. I'ar. dial.\n\nCotgravp, in v.\n\n",
